1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,988
Here's a mistake that looks like careful analysis and isn't.

2
00:00:03,988 --> 00:00:08,961
Someone runs a cold-audience awareness test, gets a strong click-through rate, and

3
00:00:08,961 --> 00:00:09,871
calls it a win.

4
00:00:09,871 --> 00:00:12,357
The click-through rate really was strong.

5
00:00:12,357 --> 00:00:15,813
It just wasn't the thing that test was supposed to prove.

6
00:00:15,813 --> 00:00:18,300
Every metric answers a specific question.

7
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:23,151
Read the wrong metric and you'll get a confident, precise, completely irrelevant

8
00:00:23,151 --> 00:00:23,575
answer.

9
00:00:23,575 --> 00:00:25,577
The fix isn't a better dashboard.

10
00:00:25,577 --> 00:00:30,610
It's knowing which number actually maps to what you were testing before you look at

11
00:00:30,610 --> 00:00:31,337
any of them.

12
00:00:31,337 --> 00:00:35,340
Why does this trap catch experienced people as often as beginners?

13
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:39,099
Because every number on a results page looks equally official.

14
00:00:39,099 --> 00:00:44,133
A dashboard doesn't label one metric "relevant to your actual question" and another

15
00:00:44,133 --> 00:00:48,923
"impressive but beside the point." That judgement has to come from you, decided

16
00:00:48,923 --> 00:00:53,956
before the numbers arrive, the same discipline Section 7.2 already asked of budgets

17
00:00:53,956 --> 00:00:54,866
and kill rules.

18
00:00:55,416 --> 00:01:00,114
Notice each row asks a genuinely different question, not a smaller version of the

19
00:01:00,114 --> 00:01:00,636
same one.

20
00:01:00,636 --> 00:01:05,450
A cold-awareness ad earning strong click-through but weak hook retention is telling

21
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:10,148
you something real: people who saw it stopped scrolling for the wrong reason, and

22
00:01:10,148 --> 00:01:13,164
it's not converting attention into anything durable.

23
00:01:13,164 --> 00:01:16,354
This maps directly onto Section 2.2's awareness ladder.

24
00:01:16,354 --> 00:01:20,530
A cold audience and a warm one aren't just different targeting settings.

25
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:25,053
They need different proof, and reading them against the same metric hides that

26
00:01:25,053 --> 00:01:27,083
difference instead of revealing it.

27
00:01:27,083 --> 00:01:30,621
Notice, too, that the three rows aren't ranked by importance.

28
00:01:30,621 --> 00:01:35,319
Cost per purchase isn't a "better" metric than hook retention, it's the right one

29
00:01:35,319 --> 00:01:36,769
for a different question.

30
00:01:36,769 --> 00:01:41,641
An awareness campaign judged on cost per purchase looks like a failure by a standard

31
00:01:41,641 --> 00:01:46,223
it was never trying to meet, and a conversion campaign judged on hook retention

32
00:01:46,223 --> 00:01:48,775
looks impressive while quietly losing money.

33
00:01:48,775 --> 00:01:53,647
If you're only running one type of campaign right now, conversion, most likely, it's

34
00:01:53,647 --> 00:01:56,257
still worth knowing the other two rows exist.

35
00:01:56,257 --> 00:02:00,955
The moment you add a genuinely cold prospecting push or a warm retargeting layer,

36
00:02:00,955 --> 00:02:05,711
you'll need a different metric on standby, not a scramble to work out which number

37
00:02:05,711 --> 00:02:08,727
actually matters once that campaign is already live.

38
00:02:09,277 --> 00:02:14,411
Same creative, two completely different reads, because it's doing two different jobs

39
00:02:14,411 --> 00:02:16,305
depending on who's watching it.

40
00:02:16,305 --> 00:02:21,439
Judging the cold-audience placement on cost per purchase is unfair to an ad that was

41
00:02:21,439 --> 00:02:24,801
never trying to close a sale in the first four seconds.

42
00:02:24,801 --> 00:02:29,630
Judging the retargeting placement on hook retention misses the actual question,

43
00:02:29,630 --> 00:02:30,852
whether it converts.

44
00:02:30,852 --> 00:02:35,069
Notice what stayed identical across both rows: the actual video file.

45
00:02:35,069 --> 00:02:39,714
Nothing about the creative itself changed between the cold placement and the

46
00:02:39,714 --> 00:02:40,692
retargeting one.

47
00:02:40,692 --> 00:02:45,093
Only the audience, the objective, and the metric that judges it changed.

48
00:02:45,093 --> 00:02:50,166
That's worth sitting with, because it means the same asset can be a genuine success

49
00:02:50,166 --> 00:02:55,239
in one context and simply irrelevant in another, without a single frame of it being

50
00:02:55,239 --> 00:02:55,850
different.

51
00:02:55,850 --> 00:03:00,067
This is why Section 7.1's isolated ad-set structure matters here too.

52
00:03:00,067 --> 00:03:04,407
Mixed objectives in one ad set make this kind of clean read impossible.

53
00:03:04,407 --> 00:03:09,052
Keep them separate, and the right metric for each one is obvious rather than

54
00:03:09,052 --> 00:03:12,047
something you have to reconstruct after the fact.

55
00:03:12,047 --> 00:03:17,119
Ask yourself, before your next test even launches: what's the one metric that would

56
00:03:17,119 --> 00:03:22,070
tell you this specific ad set worked, decided now, written down, not chosen after

57
00:03:22,070 --> 00:03:24,515
the results are already in front of you.

58
00:03:24,515 --> 00:03:29,527
If you can't answer that question before launch, you haven't actually decided what

59
00:03:29,527 --> 00:03:30,688
you're testing yet.

60
00:03:31,238 --> 00:03:34,927
One honest trap worth naming, because it's genuinely tempting.

61
00:03:34,927 --> 00:03:39,746
If cost per purchase came back disappointing but click-through rate looked great,

62
00:03:39,746 --> 00:03:43,018
it's natural to lead with the number that looks better.

63
00:03:43,018 --> 00:03:43,732
Resist that.

64
00:03:43,732 --> 00:03:48,432
The objective you set before the test started is the one that gets to judge the

65
00:03:48,432 --> 00:03:48,849
result.

66
00:03:48,849 --> 00:03:53,073
Switching which metric matters after seeing the numbers isn't analysis.

67
00:03:53,073 --> 00:03:57,357
It's choosing the answer you wanted and finding a question that fits it.

68
00:03:57,357 --> 00:04:01,879
This is the same honesty Section 7.4 asked of small sample sizes, aimed at a

69
00:04:01,879 --> 00:04:03,247
different failure mode.

70
00:04:03,247 --> 00:04:06,281
There, the risk was reading real noise as a signal.

71
00:04:06,281 --> 00:04:09,851
Here, the risk is reading the wrong signal as the right one.

72
00:04:09,851 --> 00:04:14,611
Both mistakes produce a confident, wrong conclusion, and both get fixed the same

73
00:04:14,611 --> 00:04:19,430
way: deciding what counts as evidence before you're looking at results that might

74
00:04:19,430 --> 00:04:20,917
tempt you to redefine it.

75
00:04:21,467 --> 00:04:25,962
Next lecture takes the metric you now know to read and turns it into an actual

76
00:04:25,962 --> 00:04:30,572
decision: winner, loser, or still just noise, using the same honesty Section 7.4

77
00:04:30,572 --> 00:04:32,358
already asked of small numbers.

78
00:04:32,358 --> 00:04:36,795
Before that, go back through whatever tests you already have live or recently

79
00:04:36,795 --> 00:04:40,540
finished, and check each one against the table from this lecture.

80
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:44,862
Ask, honestly, whether the metric you used to judge it actually matched the

81
00:04:44,862 --> 00:04:45,899
objective you set.

82
00:04:45,899 --> 00:04:49,760
If any of them don't line up, that's not a reason to redo the test.

83
00:04:49,760 --> 00:04:54,312
It's a reason to read it again, properly, before you draw a conclusion from it.

84
00:04:54,312 --> 00:04:58,230
Do this now, while it costs you nothing but a few minutes of review.

85
00:04:58,230 --> 00:05:02,897
The alternative is carrying a wrong conclusion forward into Section nine's weekly

86
00:05:02,897 --> 00:05:07,392
sprint, where it quietly shapes which angles you decide are worth building on.
